CONSENT_TERMS=I. MyPuppyD TERMS OF USE\n\n1. Nature of the application. MyPuppyD is software under development (open alpha). It may contain bugs, and its behaviour and feature set may change between versions. The application is provided “as is” — with no warranty that its results are correct, complete or fit for any particular purpose.\n\n2. What the application does. MyPuppyD identifies the engine control unit (ECU) from a loaded BIN file, locates maps in it, lets you view and edit them, stores project versions with a change history, and offers ready-made service solutions (DPF, EGR, DTC, AdBlue and others). Every result — identification, map addresses, values, checksums, solutions — is a suggestion that you must verify yourself.\n\n3. Your responsibility. You alone are responsible for how you use the results — in particular for flashing modified software into a vehicle — and for the compliance of such actions with applicable law (including type-approval, emissions, road-safety and manufacturer-warranty rules). To the fullest extent permitted by law, the author is not liable for damage arising from use of the application or its results; this does not exclude liability that cannot lawfully be excluded.\n\n4. Network connections. On every start the application may connect to the author's update repository and to public time-stamping authorities to check for a newer version and to verify that the current version is still valid. Without that connection the application may refuse to start. No data from your files or projects is sent in the process; the server operator receives only technical connection data (such as your IP address). A new version is downloaded and installed only after you confirm it in the update window.\n\n5. Reporting problems. The “Report a problem” button sends your report (description and any attachments you add, packed into a ZIP) only at your explicit request, over the same channel as diagnostic data (see part II).\n\n6. Diagnostic data. Automatic sending of diagnostic data is described in part II and requires a separate, voluntary consent that you can withdraw at any time in Settings (“Consents” tab). Not giving that consent does not limit the application.\n\n7. Intellectual property. The application, its detection-pattern database (delivered in encrypted form) and accompanying files are protected by copyright. They may not be decompiled, extracted or distributed outside the application, except where mandatory law permits.\n\n8. Acceptance and changes. This window appears on first launch and after every update. Using the application requires accepting these terms; without acceptance the application closes. Changed terms are presented for acceptance again.\n
CONSENT_DIAG_INFO=II. DIAGNOSTIC DATA NOTICE (optional consent)\n\nWhat is sent. With consent given, the application — only at the moment you export a file (BIN/HEX/S19) — automatically sends:\n• the project file (.prj) packed in a ZIP archive, if a custom solution was used in the project or a map file is embedded in it. The project file contains: the original ECU BIN file you loaded, the embedded map file (CSV/XDF), the list of project versions with their names and descriptions, the change history (addresses, values before and after, your descriptions) and ECU identification data read from the file (type, SW/HW numbers, Bosch number and similar);\n• a description of the custom solutions used: name/type, ECU family, byte pattern, replacement, offset and the comment entered when they were created;\n• the ECU family and the map file name (name only, no path).\nNot sent: file paths on your computer, computer name, user name, licence data or any information about your system. The transmission service operator receives only technical connection data (such as your IP address).\n\nPurpose. The data is used solely to develop the application: improving ECU and map detection, verifying service solutions and fixing bugs. It is not used for marketing or profiling.\n\nController and legal basis. The data controller is the author of MyPuppyD — CyfroTech Robert Bittner, Cyfrotech.zamuwienia@outlook.com, Rokitki Tczewskie Wiśniowa 6c. Processing is based on your consent (Art. 6(1)(a) GDPR). The ECU file and your descriptions may contain personal data (e.g. a VIN stored in the ECU memory, or details you typed into version descriptions or comments) — by sending them you confirm that you are entitled to do so.\n\nRecipients and transfers outside the EEA. The data is transmitted via the messeng to the author's private channel. Is a separate entity whose servers are also located outside the European Economic Area; the transfer is based on your explicit consent (Art. 49(1)(a) GDPR). This carries the risk that the data will not enjoy protection equivalent to the GDPR — there is no adequacy decision and no other safeguards within the meaning of Art. 46 GDPR. \n\nRetention. The data is kept for as long as it is needed for developing the application, or until you request its deletion — whichever comes first.\n\nYour rights. Consent is voluntary and you can withdraw it at any time: Settings → Consents → untick the box and save; from then on nothing is sent. Withdrawal does not affect the lawfulness of transmissions made before it. You have the right of access, rectification, erasure, restriction of processing and data portability, and the right to lodge a complaint with a supervisory authority (in Poland: the President of the Personal Data Protection Office, UODO). The data sent is not linked to your identity, so exercising rights in respect of a specific transmission may require you to identify the file or the date (Art. 11 GDPR).
